  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1... Remaining damp and cool through tonight with drier \nconditions and more sun to finish out the workweek. \n\nConditions will continue to improve tonight into Thursday as drier \nair filters in from the northeast. The CAD wedge is currently at \nit's peak yielding some lingering pockets of drizzle/light showers \nmainly along and south of I-66/US-50 into central VA and the \nsouthern Shenandoah Valley. Strong 1040 mb high pressure over \nsoutheast Quebec will continue to strengthen tonight into Thursday \nallowing more dry air into the region. At the same time, low \npressure will begin to strengthen off the Delmarva/NC coast before \nriding north toward southern New England as a pretty impressive \nNor'aster Friday into Saturday. \n\nFor the remainder of the afternoon and evening, expect pockets of \ndrizzle and passing showers confined to locations west of the Blue \nRidge and down into central VA. Elsewhere conditions will remain \nmostly dry with overcast conditions continuing. Some brightening in \nthe clouds may be noted up across northeast MD as drier air \ncontinues to filter in. Highs this afternoon will peak in the mid \n50s to mid 60s with overnight lows in tonight into the upper 40s and \nmid 50s. Winds will remain gusty out of the northeast, with gusts to \naround 20-30 mph possible at times to the east of the Blue Ridge.\n\nDrier weather returns Thursday and Friday although breezy conditions \nwill remain as the upper level trough axis passes overhead. This \nwill lead to more clouds than sunshine Thursday with better clearing \nFriday as more subsidence works into the region. Coastal low \npressure will continue to deepen off the Delmarva coast leading to \ngusty north to northeast winds for both Thursday and Friday \nafternoon. Gale conditions will be likely across the open and middle \nwaters as well as out along the Atlantic coast given the tightened \npressure gradient.  Temperatures will stay below normal as a result, \nwith highs in the 60s tomorrow, and then upper 60s to near 70 on \nFriday.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Breezy and warmer this weekend, but forecast remains \nuncertain as Nor'easter impacts Northeast U.S. \n\nAnomalously strong surface ridge (by late September standards) \nfinally weakens this weekend as a strong Nor'easter develops off the \ncoast of Southern New England/LI. Ensemble members still show quite \na bit of spread regarding the exact track of the Nor'easter. A few \nmembers show the possibility the low tracks closer to the NJ / \nDelmarva coast this weekend. Regardless of exact position, we are \nlikely to be on the drier (subsidence) side of the system with \nnorthwest winds bringing in breezy, warmer, and mostly dry \nconditions. If the Nor'easter tracks closer to our area that could \nbring some rain chances to the area, especially along/east of I-95, \nalong with the potential for increasing winds. Safe to say that \nthere isn't a definite trend for this weekend, so be sure to keep \ntrack of the forecast for any changes in the coming days.\n\nThe Nor'easter is eventually pushed offshore at the start of next \nweek as high pressure builds in from the OH Valley and Great Lakes. \nMid-level ridging builds over much of the eastern CONUS during the \nmiddle of next week, marking the return of much warmer temperatures \nand continued dry conditions.",
